  "title": "Typical tactics and plans",
  "intro": "Six positions from the lines you have just learnt, each stopping on the move that matters. Play through the moves and find the last one.\n\n## What these are\n\nThree of them end on a capture that wins material, and three end on a decision. That is roughly the balance of the opening itself: the Exchange is a structural choice with a handful of concrete moments in it, and the concrete moments nearly all come from the same question.\n\n## The question\n\nWhat is defending e5, and what is looking at d1.\n\nHalf of this course is one habit: before you take on e5, count the defenders, and before you take anything, look down the diagonal that ends at your own queen. The 5...Ne7 line is a free pawn because nothing defends e5. The 5...Bg4 line loses a queen for the same capture, because the bishop has been staring at d1 since move five. The move is identical. The position is not.\n\n## The other three\n\nDo not take the bishop on g4 after ...h5, because the file Black has opened is worth more than the piece. Recapture on f3 with the queen and not the g-pawn, in every line, every time. And in the main line, when the pieces are placed and the queens are off, the move is still development rather than a pawn: the majority goes last.\n\n## Typical mistakes at this level\n\nTaking a piece because it is there. Grabbing the h-file bishop and then finding the knight on f3 cannot move. Recapturing towards the centre out of habit and wrecking the majority. And pushing the kingside pawns before the pieces behind them are ready.",
